<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Is Renewable Energy?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Clarify written instructions for peers. Communication: Participate in conflict resolution strategies with peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help clarify misunderstandings about energy sources by combining gestures and visual aids to make complex instructions easier to understand (e.g., using hand motions to show how solar panels collect sunlight alongside pictures or diagrams).
                Can mediate disagreements in group projects by suggesting taking turns and encouraging raising their hands to ask questions (e.g., suggests each peer takes turns sharing their energy-saving ideas, or says, "Why don't we all raise our hands to ask questions before voting on the best idea?").
                Can help resolve misunderstandings about energy sources by using gestures alone to explain key concepts (e.g., using hand movements to demonstrate how a dam holds water to generate electricity).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a complex diagram of how a hydropower plant works to generate electricity. Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what they understand and what parts confuse them. The teacher encourages questions, highlighting the need for clarification and understanding. (e.g., "What part of this diagram for generating power is unclear?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify information using simple language and gestures. For example, pointing to a turbine in the diagram and using hand motions to show "converting water's energy into electricity." The teacher also models how to mediate a simple disagreement about the diagram's interpretation by saying, "Let's first clarify what each part does before we discuss its efficiency." Focus on stress in "renewable" and "preservation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't understand 'harnessing wind power,' what can you do to help them?" (Elicits: explain, rephrase, show gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to justify your opinion when discussing renewable energy?" (Elicits: so others can understand, to make your point clear).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short set of written instructions (e.g., "How to design an effective Renewable Energy Infographic") outlining key steps and technical terms. The instructions include concepts such as "visualizing data efficiently," "highlighting sustainability benefits," and "integrating local context of Panama." Some vocabulary (e.g., "infrastructure," "consumption," "reliance") is subtly embedded.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explaining environmental benefits and justifying opinions. Students will also focus on active listening to identify parts requiring clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the instructions individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of these instructions?" "What are three key elements for designing an infographic about clean energy?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Targeted Questions for Mediation:</b> "Which part of the instructions about 'preserving natural resources' or 'reducing consumption' might be confusing for someone who doesn't know much about renewable energy?" "Are there any terms you would need to simplify for a classmate?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using gerunds to describe environmental actions (e.g., "Saving energy is important."), using the present continuous to describe ongoing actions (e.g., "We are installing solar panels."), and using adverbial clauses for justification (e.g., "Renewable energy is vital because it preserves natural resources."). Vocabulary: energy, source, electricity, preservation, sustainability, efficiency, turbine, panels, fossil fuels, consume, generate, preserve, justify.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Clarifying Jargon (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a list of complex terms from the infographic instructions (e.g., "grid efficiency," "carbon footprint reduction," "sustainable infrastructure"). For each term, partners take turns defining it in simple language, using a gerund phrase (e.g., "Reducing the carbon footprint is preventing pollution.") and practicing pronunciation, focusing on stress in "sustainability" and "environment."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Explaining Steps (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> Students are given a sequence of steps for designing a specific section of their infographic (e.g., "1. Choose an energy source. 2. Find relevant data. 3. Design visual elements."). In pairs, Student A explains a step to Student B, using the present continuous (e.g., "First, you are choosing your main energy source, like wind or solar.") Student B asks for clarification, and Student A rephrases using an adverbial clause for justification (e.g., "We need to choose carefully because it defines the whole infographic.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Mediating Misunderstandings (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> The teacher presents a few deliberately ambiguous statements or conflicting ideas related to the infographic project (e.g., "Is 'preserving resources' the same as 'saving energy'?" or "Should we focus only on solar panels or include other energy sources from Panama?"). In pairs, students discuss these points. They practice mediating the discussion by suggesting conflict resolution strategies like "Let's take turns explaining our points," or "Why don't we raise our hands to ask questions about the infographic design?" The goal is to reach a mutual understanding by clarifying different perspectives.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Infographic Clarification & Peer Mediation Session." Students work in groups of three. Each group brings a draft section of their Renewable Energy Infographic. One student (Presenter) explains a specific aspect of the infographic (e.g., "How solar panels are generating electricity in Panama" or "The benefits of wind power for sustainability"). Another student (Challenger) pretends to misunderstand or disagrees with a point made, asking for clarification. The third student (Mediator) intervenes to clarify the information, rephrase complex ideas, or mediate the disagreement, ensuring understanding for both peers. They should use gestures and simple explanations to make concepts clear. They must use gerunds (e.g., "Understanding hydropower is key..."), present continuous (e.g., "We are showing how Panama is investing in clean energy..."), and adverbial clauses for justification (e.g., "The infographic highlights wind power because it is a sustainable resource in certain regions.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Circulate, observe, and provide support. Encourage natural interaction and focus on effective mediation strategies. Remind students to provide solutions and ideas related to Panama's energy use, justifying their suggestions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "Mediation Challenge." The teacher presents a scenario with a common misunderstanding or minor disagreement related to renewable energy or the infographic project (e.g., "One peer thinks 'fossil fuels' are a type of renewable energy, another peer thinks the infographic should only show text, not images."). Students individually write down (or briefly present) how they would mediate this situation, clarifying the misunderstanding or resolving the disagreement using appropriate mediation phrases and target language. They must explain why their approach is effective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of the mediated explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of gestures and/or visual aids (if applicable in presentation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective application of conflict resolution strategies (e.g., suggesting turns, asking questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of gerunds, present continuous, and adverbial clauses for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words like "sustainability."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new strategy you learned today for clarifying information or mediating a disagreement?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about helping a peer understand a complex energy concept?" (Scale of 1-5).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of mediating discussions about our Renewable Energy Infographic, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what other school or life situations could you use the mediation skills you practiced today, especially when explaining or justifying ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation activities allowed students to apply all previous language skills in a communicative context. Students effectively used gestures and rephrasing to clarify instructions for their infographic, demonstrating progress in their A2.4 mediation competencies. The practice with conflict resolution strategies (taking turns, raising hands) was particularly useful. Some students still need more practice with adverbial clauses for justifying their clarifications. Next time, I will provide more sentence starters for justification during the preparation stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "What Is Renewable Energy?" and the 21st Century Skills Project on infographics. Students are now equipped to effectively present and clarify their infographic content in a future class, or they can apply these mediation skills to the next theme's collaborative project work, fostering stronger group dynamics and deeper understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>